   "id": "math-0016",
   "bucket": "reasoning",
   "planned_path": "P5",
   "scion_answer": "We can use the **binomial probability formula**:\n\n$$P(X = k) = \\binom{n}{k} p^k (1-p)^{n-k}$$\n\nwhere:\n- $n = 5$ (number of trials)\n- $k = 2$ (number of heads desired)\n- $p = \\dfrac{2}{3}$ (probability of heads)\n- $1 - p = \\dfrac{1}{3}$ (probability of tails)\n\n**Step 1: Compute the binomial coefficient**\n\n$$\\binom{5}{2} = \\frac{5!}{2! \\cdot 3!} = \\frac{120}{2 \\cdot 6} = 10$$\n\n**Step 2: Compute the probability terms**\n\n$$p^2 = \\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)^2 = \\frac{4}{9}$$\n\n$$q^3 = \\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)^3 = \\frac{1}{27}$$\n\n**Step 3: Multiply everything together**\n\n$$P(X = 2) = 10 \\cdot \\frac{4}{9} \\cdot \\frac{1}{27} = \\frac{40}{243}$$\n\nThe probability of getting exactly two heads in 5 flips is:\n\n$$\\boxed{\\dfrac{40}{243}}$$",
